  *Abstract.* Maps of the South Atlantic Anomaly (SAA) built from low-Earth-orbit particle data depend on choices that are rarely reported together: how a "high-flux" region is thresholded, which energy channel and cell statistic are used, how long observations are accumulated, and which spacecraft supplies them. We quantify these dependencies for one month, January 2024, using NOAA/NCEI processed SEM-2 MEPED omnidirectional differential proton fluxes (nominal 25, 50 and 100 MeV) from NOAA-15, NOAA-18, NOAA-19, MetOp-01 and MetOp-03. A candidate footprint is defined as the cells at or above a percentile of a coverage-gated latitude–longitude grid, and is summarized by spherical selected area and a flux-and-area-weighted coordinate centroid. With the reference setting (NOAA-19, p1, 5#sq cells, cell mean, top 10%) the centroid lies at #val("p_lat")#sq, #val("p_lon")#sq. Tightening the threshold from the top 20% to the top 1% moves the centroid by #val("thr_shift_min")–#val("thr_shift_max") km across grids and statistics; the accompanying #val("thr_area_ratio_5_mean")-fold area contraction is almost entirely prescribed by the percentile rule. For the top-10% footprint, the p3 centroid lies #val("ch_d_p3p1") km from p1, south and east of it, and this direction persisted across paired 1-, 3- and 7-day calendar-block resampling. For a three-day window, the end-to-end area-weighted Jaccard index with the monthly map is #val("tw_d3_Je") and the centroid distance #val("tw_d3_De") km; recomputed on the cells eligible in both maps, the footprints agree at #val("tw_d3_Jc") and #val("tw_d3_Dc") km, whereas a one-day map still differs on that shared support (#val("tw_d1_Dc") km). A quality rule adopted after inspecting the data excludes a fallback-fit OMNI value only when it exceeds both neighbouring stretches of regular fits a hundredfold. Across #val("qc_records") native records it excludes one NOAA-15 record, whose >35 MeV count rate was #val("rc_p7p6") times its simultaneous >16 MeV rate. With this rule the five-satellite maximum centroid separation is #val("sat_max_prim") km (#val("sat_max_median_prim") km with cell medians); without it, the cell-mean value is #val("sat_max_unf") km. The contribution is a reproducible accounting of how mapping choices, sampling support and individual observations enter a derived SAA location; it is not a validated physical model of the anomaly.
]

= Introduction

The South Atlantic Anomaly is the region where the inner proton belt reaches its lowest altitudes over South America and the South Atlantic, so that satellites in low Earth orbit encounter elevated energetic-particle fluxes there. Its location and extent are commonly described from particle measurements rather than from the magnetic field alone. Long particle records have been used to follow the SAA's slow westward drift and its solar-cycle modulation in location, area and peak intensity: from the particle monitor on the Rossi X-ray Timing Explorer @furst2009rxte, from particle-noise pulses in an ultraviolet photometer @schaefer2016saa, from NOAA-15 proton data fitted with Gaussian forms @qin2014solarcycle, and from NOAA/MetOp SEM-2 proton fluxes over 1998–2022 @ginisty2024saa. Shorter-term changes have been mapped by pooling several NOAA and MetOp spacecraft; #cite(<xu2025storm>, form: "prose"), for example, used a three-day running average of integral proton channels to resolve a storm-time decrease that month-long averages would smooth.

Each of these descriptions is an estimate produced by a pipeline. A particle-defined "SAA region" depends on how flux is gridded, what counts as high flux, which energy channel and cell statistic are used, how long data are accumulated, and which spacecraft sampled the region. These choices are usually fixed once and reported in passing. When they are varied at all, the variation is typically one factor at a time. It is therefore hard to know how much of a reported location or area belongs to the particle environment and how much to the analysis.

This paper asks a narrower question than SAA physics. *For one month of public NOAA/NCEI SEM-2 data, how much do the location and extent of a percentile-defined candidate high-flux footprint change under controlled changes of threshold, cell statistic and grid, proton channel, accumulation window and spacecraft, and which of those changes are explained by sampling support or by individual observations rather than by the flux field?* January 2024 is used as a single, documented case.

We make three contributions. First, we define the footprint estimands (selected set, spherical area and coordinate-moment centroid) and their denominators explicitly, and we separate the parts of each comparison that the definition itself prescribes. Second, we compare short-window maps with the monthly map in two ways: end to end, using the original footprints, and on shared eligible support, after recomputing each map's percentile on the cells both maps can support. Third, we trace a large inter-satellite difference to one source record, check it against the provider's count-rate file, and exclude it under a stated post-hoc quality rule while reporting the unfiltered result alongside. Channel differences are examined with a paired calendar-block resampling protocol fixed before production resampling. All values in this paper are generated from a machine-readable claims register tied to hashed outputs, and the complete computation is reproducible from the raw daily files.

The results are case-study sensitivities. They do not define a true SAA boundary or centre, do not measure dose or hazard, and do not establish physical motion of the anomaly.

= Data and methods

== Product and provenance

We use the processed SEM-2 daily NetCDF files distributed by NOAA's National Centers for Environmental Information @ncei_sem2 for all 31 days of January 2024 from NOAA-15, NOAA-18, NOAA-19, MetOp-01 and MetOp-03 (155 files; SHA-256 manifest in Supplement S1). The archive directory is named `l1b/v01r00`. The files themselves declare `processing_level = "Level 2, calibrated fluxes"`, consistent with the NOAA processing description, in which level-1B sensor data are converted to physical units and supplemented with field-model parameters @green2013manual. We therefore describe the product by its file metadata, not by the directory name. The file title also states that the data have known contamination problems.

The analysed variables are `mep_omni_flux_p1`, `p2` and `p3`. According to the OMNI flux algorithm description @machol2012atbd, count rates from four overlapping omnidirectional channels (16–35, 35–250, 70–250 and 140–250 MeV) are redistributed into non-overlapping bands. A piecewise power law in energy is then fitted, and the fitted differential flux is evaluated at 25, 50 and 100 MeV (p1, p2, p3). The file metadata give these variables as differential proton flux at 25, 50 and 100 MeV in `#/cm2-s-str-MeV`. The user manual's variable table prints the unit without the per-energy factor @green2013manual. We follow the algorithm definition and the file metadata and report flux in cm#super[−2] s#super[−1] sr#super[−1] MeV#super[−1]. The three channels are therefore *fitted outputs from shared counts and a shared spectral model*, not independent instruments. NOAA's calibration and processing are inputs to this study; we perform no cross-satellite or cross-channel intensity calibration of our own.

== Quality handling and record accounting

Records are restricted to latitude −70#sq to 20#sq and longitude −100#sq to 20#sq (longitudes converted from [0#sq, 360#sq) to [−180#sq, 180#sq)). Rows with the in-flight-calibration flag `mep_IFC_on = 1` are removed (#val("ifc_on_noaa15") NOAA-15 regional record; none on the other spacecraft). The flag's documented values are 0 and 1, yet most regional records carry −1 (e.g. #val("ifc_minus1_noaa19") of #val("rows_noaa19") NOAA-19 records). Its meaning is not documented, so these records are retained and left uninterpreted. Following the algorithm's fit-flag definition (−1 = no fit performed) and the documented minimum of zero for the OMNI fluxes @machol2012atbd @green2013manual, a channel value is set missing when it is negative or its fit flag is −1. Valid zeros and fit types 0–2 are retained; no upper cutoff or automatic despiking is applied. In the study region this affects #val("invalid_p1_total") p1 values (#val("invalid_p1_noaa15") NOAA-15, #val("invalid_p1_noaa18") NOAA-18, none on the other spacecraft). Missing values are never interpolated or zero-filled. Supplement S2 gives the per-satellite accounting.

Only the 31 NOAA-19 files were cached with recorded hashes before this analysis was finalized. The other 124 January files were retrieved again from the same archive, so byte hashes of the files originally used for some earlier project checkpoints are not available. Regional tables rebuilt from the retrieved files reproduce the saved values exactly, which supports value lineage but does not recover the unrecorded historical bytes.

*Post-hoc quality rule QC-1.* The OMNI algorithm fits separate power laws over four energy ranges (fit flag 0) when the count rates allow it. Otherwise it falls back to a single power law built from the two lowest channels (flag 2) or from one anchor with the default slope $gamma = -2.9$ (flag 1) @machol2012atbd. This fallback is a documented fit mode and is common at low count rates, so we do not treat it as invalid. After the satellite comparison had drawn attention to one NOAA-15 record (Sect. 2.8), we adopted the following rule and applied it to every native record of the 155 files. A record's OMNI values are set missing when (i) its fit is a fallback (flag 1 or 2); (ii) its #val("qc_k") preceding and #val("qc_k") following native records are contiguous in time (every step positive and at most #val("qc_step") s, three nominal cadences) and all received the regular fit; and (iii) for at least one channel, all neighbour values are finite and positive and the record's value exceeds #val("qc_ratio") times the median of the preceding records and #val("qc_ratio") times the median of the following records. The three channel values of a flagged record come from one fit and are excluded together. Values are never clipped or replaced, records that cannot be evaluated are kept, and the source files are not altered.

Condition (ii) restricts the rule to stretches where the counts supported the regular fit, which the algorithm attempts only when the summed raw count rate is at least 25 s#super[−1] @machol2012atbd. There, record-to-record variation is small: across #val("qc_regular_n") evaluable regular-fit records, no p1 value departs from both neighbourhoods by more than a factor of #val("qc_regular_max"). The documented fallback itself departs further, because one power law with a fixed slope does not follow the local spectrum: in p1, the #val("qc_iso_fallback") isolated fallback records sit a median factor of #val("qc_fallback_median_min")–#val("qc_fallback_median_max") above their regular-fit neighbours (by satellite), and at most #val("qc_fallback_max"). The ratio must exceed that range, or the rule would reject a documented fit mode for its known bias; #val("qc_ratio") is #val("qc_ratio_margin") times its upper end. Neither the NOAA documentation nor the literature we found gives an automatic criterion for single-record departures: the provider's quality assessment is flag-based @machol2012atbd, and a cross-calibration of the same >16 MeV omnidirectional data removed scattered erroneous points by manual inspection @lin2019crosscal. QC-1 is therefore a post-hoc decision rule, stated in full so that it can be replayed. In a perturbation study (Supplement S7), #val("qc_pert_same") of #val("qc_pert_n") parameter variants give exactly the same excluded set, including every ratio from #val("qc_ratio_lo") to #val("qc_ratio_hi"). The set changes only when the ratio drops into the fallback's own departure range (#val("qc_ratio_15_n") records at 15×, #val("qc_ratio_10_n") at 10×) or when condition (ii) is dropped (#val("qc_nonb_n") records, mostly low-flux fallback values beside near-zero neighbours). QC-1 excludes #val("qc_excl_global") record of #val("qc_records") (Sect. 3.5); every other fallback value is retained.

== Coverage-gated grids

Valid values are binned by sub-satellite latitude and longitude into 5#sq and 2#sq cells. The nominal grids have 432 and 2,700 cells. Each cell receives its sample count, mean and median. A cell is *eligible* for a monthly map when it has at least 30 valid samples. Shorter windows of $d$ days use $max(3, "round"(30 d \/ 31))$ samples, i.e. 3, 3, 7, 14 and 30 for 1, 3, 7, 14 and 31 days. In January, #val("eligible_5") of 432 5#sq cells and #val("eligible_2") of 2,700 2#sq cells are eligible for NOAA-19 p1. Ineligible cells carry no statistic and never enter a footprint. We distinguish cells with *no valid observation*, cells *observed but below the sample minimum*, and cells *eligible but not selected*.

== Footprint, area and centroid

Let $E$ be the eligible cells of one map and $F_i$ the gridded statistic (mean or median) of cell $i$. For a percentile $q in {80, 90, 95, 98, 99}$ ("top 20%" … "top 1%"), the cutoff $c_q$ is the linear-interpolation $q$-th percentile of ${F_i : i in E}$, and the footprint is
$ S_q = {i in E : F_i >= c_q}. $ <eq-select>
The percentile is taken over eligible *cells*, not over equal areas or individual records. Cell $i$ spanning latitudes $phi_i^-$ to $phi_i^+$ and longitude width $Delta lambda$ has spherical area $A_i = R^2 Delta lambda (sin phi_i^+ - sin phi_i^-)$ with $R = 6371$ km. The selected area is $A(S_q) = sum_(i in S_q) A_i$. The centroid is the coordinate moment
$ (overline(phi), overline(lambda)) = (sum_(i in S_q) w_i (phi_i, lambda_i)) / (sum_(i in S_q) w_i), quad w_i = F_i A_i, $ <eq-centroid>
with cell-centre coordinates $(phi_i, lambda_i)$. It is a latitude–longitude moment, valid here because the region does not cross the antimeridian. It is not a three-dimensional barycentre, and its value depends on the chosen region. Distances between centroids are great-circle (haversine) distances. For two footprints $X$, $Y$ on the *same aligned grid*, the area-weighted Jaccard index is
$ J(X, Y) = A(X inter Y) \/ A(X union Y), $ <eq-jaccard>
and it is undefined when both sets are empty. Cells of different resolutions are never compared as pixels.

== Controlled comparisons

Table 1 summarizes the design. Each family changes one focal factor and crosses it with the same grid × statistic × threshold settings; factors are not combined into new joint experiments. The canonical configuration universe has #val("cfg_total") entries (#val("cfg_threshold") threshold, #val("cfg_channel") channel, #val("cfg_time") time-window and #val("cfg_satellite") satellite). They are not independent. The NOAA-19 monthly p1 map appears in all four families, and the first calendar week is the same date range as the cumulative seven-day window. Only #val("cfg_distinct") of the #val("cfg_total") configurations are distinct (Supplement S3).

