== Temporal coverage versus conditional morphology

A short-window footprint $F_W$ can differ from the January footprint $F_M$ because the field differs or because the window supports fewer cells. Let $C_W$ and $C_M$ be the eligible cells of window $W$ and of January at the same grid, statistic and threshold. We report (i) the eligible area fraction of each window; (ii) the *end-to-end* comparison of the original footprints, $J(F_W, F_M)$, centroid distance and area ratio; and (iii) the *monthly-footprint observability*
$O = A(F_M inter C_W) \/ A(F_M)$, which measures how much of the monthly selected area meets the window's eligibility rule, not how well the footprints agree. (iv) On the pair's shared support $S = C_W inter C_M$, we recompute *each* map's own percentile cutoff over $S$ under the same convention and compare the resulting *conditional* footprints with $J$, centroid distance and area ratio. $S$ is formed separately for each window–reference pair; no global intersection across windows is used, and masks are not simply intersected. Empty support, empty union or zero total weight return an explicit undefined value with a reason, never a zero or a perfect score. We also count distinct UTC days with valid data per cell as a description of sampling (not a filter; a record count is not a count of independent passes). The month contains every window, so all agreement with it is descriptive, not independent validation. These comparisons are descriptive; they do not attribute differences to sampling or to physical evolution.

== Channel stability and continuous-field summaries

Channel footprints are compared by signed centroid differences (second channel minus first) and by distance. To describe how stable these differences are to day-to-day sampling, a protocol fixed before execution resamples calendar days in blocks of 1, 3 or 7 consecutive UTC days, with replacement and without wrap. Draws are concatenated and trimmed to 31 day-slots, 1,000 replicates per block length, seed 20260929 (PCG64), and the *same* day draws are used for all three channels. In every replicate, coverage, percentiles, footprints and centroids are recomputed. Moving-block resampling was developed for stationary dependent series @kunsch1989block, and its consistency under some periodic non-stationarity has been studied for mean-type statistics @synowiecki2007mbb. Neither result covers a 31-day, coverage-gated, thresholded centroid. The quantiles are therefore reported as *conditional resampling stability*, not as calibrated confidence intervals for a physical position, and a positive distance quantile is not evidence of physical significance.

As a threshold-free complement, each channel's field on the common eligible support is normalized as
$ p_(c,i) = F_(c,i) A_i \/ sum_j F_(c,j) A_j, quad "overlap"(c, d) = sum_i min(p_(c,i), p_(d,i)), $ <eq-overlap>
which is invariant to any positive scale factor per channel. The overlap is a normalized mass overlap. It is not a footprint Jaccard index and not a "fraction of the SAA shared". The whole-region moment of $p$ is a field summary, not an SAA centre. It is a different statistic from the footprint centroid, and the two distances are not additive components of one displacement.

== The influential NOAA-15 record

A review of the principal satellite comparison found that one NOAA-15 record strongly influenced its cell mean. The record was traced to file `poes_n15_20240119_proc.nc` (SHA-256 prefix `1209804e`), zero-based native index #val("ev_index"), #val("ev_utc"), with stored p1 = #val("ev_p1"), whereas its 20 native neighbours (±20 s) have a median p1 of #val("ev_nb_p1_median"). It carries OMNI fit flag #val("ev_fit"), while all #val("ev_nb_fit0") neighbours carry flag 0. Its three channel values stand in the ratio #val("ev_spectral_ratio") between energies a factor two apart, which is $2^(2.9)$, the default slope: they are one value extended with a fixed spectral shape, not three independent enhancements. In the same record, the co-located directional MEPED P6 proton fluxes (0#sq and 90#sq telescopes), which are at least #val("ev_dir_p6_nb_min") cm#super[−2] s#super[−1] sr#super[−1] on every neighbour, are exactly zero.

To see where the stored flux came from, we read NOAA's level-1a companion file for that day, which holds the OMNI count rates from which the fluxes are fitted (retrieved for this check only; SHA-256 in Supplement S1). In this record the >35 MeV count rate is #val("rc_p7") s#super[−1], against #val("rc_p7_nb_min")–#val("rc_p7_nb_max") s#super[−1] on the neighbours and at most #val("rc_p7_second") s#super[−1] anywhere else that day. The >16 MeV rate is #val("rc_p6") s#super[−1], inside its neighbours' range (#val("rc_p6_nb_min")–#val("rc_p6_nb_max") s#super[−1]). Every proton above 35 MeV is also above 16 MeV, so these two rates cannot both describe the same particle population. Under the documented procedure, such a count-rate vector precludes the regular fit and leaves the one-point default-slope fit, consistent with the stored flag and slopes. The checks show that the record's flux values do not follow from its own simultaneous >16 MeV measurement. They do not identify the instrumental or telemetry cause.

QC-1 (Sect. 2.2) was written after this record was found; the record satisfies it and is excluded from the primary analysis. Every setting is also recomputed without QC-1 and reported as the *unfiltered* sensitivity. An earlier exact-record omission (matched by source hash and native index) is reproduced exactly by the rule-based exclusion (Supplement S7). A narrow two-sided triage run before QC-1 existed is kept in the supplement as historical context; it removed nothing.

== Reproduction and software

The pipeline uses Python with NumPy @harris2020numpy, pandas, xarray/netCDF4 and Matplotlib @hunter2007matplotlib; coastlines are from Natural Earth @naturalearth. One command regenerates every derived table, the resampling, the diagnostics, the figures, the claims register, this document and its supplement from the hashed raw files (Section 6).

= Results

== Reference footprint

For the reference setting (Fig. 1a), the top-10% cutoff is #val("p_cut") cm#super[−2] s#super[−1] sr#super[−1] MeV#super[−1]. The footprint contains #val("p_cells") of #val("eligible_5") eligible 5#sq cells (#val("p_area") × 10#super[6] km#super[2]), and its centroid is at #val("p_lat")#sq latitude, #val("p_lon")#sq longitude. The highest cell mean (#val("p_peak")) is in the adjacent cell centred at #val("p_peak_lat")#sq, #val("p_peak_lon")#sq. Every 5#sq cell meets the monthly sample minimum (Fig. 1b). At 2#sq, #val("below_min_2") observed cells fall below it and are left blank, and #val("unobserved_2") cells lack observations entirely (Fig. 1c). The selected sector over South America and the South Atlantic persists in every setting examined below. What changes is its extent and the location of its summary centroid.

