== Threshold and estimator

Tightening the percentile from top 20% to top 1% moves the reference centroid by #val("thr_shift_5_mean") km, mostly southward (Fig. 2a). Across the four grid × statistic combinations the displacement is #val("thr_shift_min")–#val("thr_shift_max") km. The path is not monotone: the successive steps on the 5#sq mean grid are #val("thr_step_top20_top10"), #val("thr_step_top10_top5"), #val("thr_step_top5_top2") and #val("thr_step_top2_top1") km, and the last step selects only five cells. Mean and median grids follow similar, not identical, paths.

Area behaves differently. Selected area falls by a factor of #val("thr_area_ratio_5_mean") between top 20% and top 1% (#val("thr_area_ratio_2_mean") at 2#sq), but the percentile rule already prescribes a nominal factor of 20 in selected *cells*. The realized cell-count ratio is #val("thr_count_ratio_5_mean"), departing from 20 only through discreteness and ties. The area ratio departs from the cell ratio only because cell areas vary with latitude. Figure 2b shows the selected areas close to the line fixed by the nominal fraction. The area contraction is therefore a property of the definition, not an independent empirical finding. Nested-threshold overlap is likewise largely fixed by the nesting of percentile sets. The informative part of the threshold family is *where* the tighter sets fall, not how large they are.

