== Accumulation window

One-day maps are sparse: the 1 January NOAA-19 map has #val("tw_d1_covN") eligible 5#sq cells (#val("tw_d1_covA")% of the regional area), and a median eligible cell was sampled on #val("tw_d1_days") day, by construction. Eligible area rises to #val("tw_d3_covA")% for three days and #val("tw_d7_covA")% for seven (Fig. 4a); at 2#sq the corresponding fractions are #val("tw2_d1_covA")%, #val("tw2_d3_covA")% and #val("tw2_d7_covA")%.

End to end, the three-day top-10% footprint differs strongly from January: area-weighted Jaccard #val("tw_d3_Je"), centroid distance #val("tw_d3_De") km (Fig. 4b,c). Only #val("tw_d3_Obs")% of the January footprint's area is even eligible in the three-day map. Restricted to the #val("tw_d3_Sn") cells eligible in both maps, with each map's top-10% recomputed there, the conditional footprints agree at Jaccard #val("tw_d3_Jc") and #val("tw_d3_Dc") km. For one day the end-to-end values are #val("tw_d1_Je") and #val("tw_d1_De") km and the conditional values #val("tw_d1_Jc") and #val("tw_d1_Dc") km. A substantial conditional difference therefore remains for one day. For seven days, end-to-end agreement is already high (#val("tw_d7_Je"), #val("tw_d7_De") km); conditioning changes little (#val("tw_d7_Jc"), #val("tw_d7_Dc") km). Over all #val("tw_nonmonth_n") non-reference rows (8 windows, counting the week-1 alias, × 20 settings), the shared-support Jaccard index is at least the end-to-end value in #val("tw_jc_ge_je_n"), but the shared-support centroid distance is *larger* in #val("tw_dc_gt_de_n") rows. Conditioning on support does not uniformly improve agreement, and no row was undefined.

Grid resolution matters. At 2#sq the three-day end-to-end Jaccard index is only #val("tw2_d3_Je") (conditional #val("tw2_d3_Jc")), and the one-day 2#sq footprint has an end-to-end Jaccard index of #val("tw2_d1_Je") yet a centroid only #val("tw2_d1_De") km from January's. Centroid distance alone can therefore understate footprint disagreement. The disjoint weeks are mutually consistent at the reference setting: pairwise week-to-week centroid distances are #val("tw_week_min")–#val("tw_week_max") km, and the second week is the most distant from January (#val("tw_w2_De") km end to end, #val("tw_w2_Dc") km conditional). The 14-day map is nearly identical to January (#val("tw_d14_De") km). This is expected, since it comprises almost half of the month.

These results describe January 2024 sampling by one spacecraft. At the reference setting, most of the three-day end-to-end difference does not persist on shared support, whereas the one-day difference does; they do not identify a universal accumulation time. They also do not separate sampling from genuine day-to-day change: the conditional comparison removes the eligibility difference only, and the month used as reference contains each window.

