== Spacecraft

With QC-1 applied, the five spacecraft give principal centroids within a few degrees of one another (Fig. 5a). The largest separation is #val("sat_max_prim") km (#val("sat_max_pair_prim")\; #val("sat_max_prim_2") km at 2#sq), the NOAA-18 and NOAA-19 centroids are #val("sat_n18n19") km apart, and the largest separation among the four spacecraft other than NOAA-15 is #val("sat_max_excl15") km. With cell medians the maximum is #val("sat_max_median_prim") km. On the 5#sq mean grid the five-satellite maximum stays between #val("sat_thr_prim_min") and #val("sat_thr_prim_max") km over the five thresholds; with medians it reaches #val("sat_med_thr_prim_max") km at the top #val("sat_med_thr_prim_argmax")% (Fig. 5b), so neither statistic gives the smaller spread at every threshold.

QC-1 excludes one record of the analysed data, the NOAA-15 record of 19 January described in Sect. 2.8, and its exclusion changes NOAA-15 products only. In its 5#sq cell (centred at −12.5#sq, −27.5#sq) the record supplied #val("ev_frac_sum")% of the summed flux of #val("ev_count_unf") values. It raised the cell mean from #val("ev_mean_prim") to #val("ev_mean_unf"), and the cell's rank among NOAA-15's eligible cells from #val("ev_rank_prim") to #val("ev_rank_unf"), while the cell median (#val("ev_median_unf")) does not change. In the unfiltered data, that cell carries #val("ev_weight")% of the NOAA-15 principal centroid weight. The NOAA-15 centroid then lies #val("n15_shift") km further east (#val("n15_lat_unf")#sq, #val("n15_lon_unf")#sq instead of #val("n15_lat_prim")#sq, #val("n15_lon_prim")#sq), and the five-satellite maximum is #val("sat_max_unf") km (#val("sat_max_pair_unf")\; #val("sat_max_unf_2") km at 2#sq). The unfiltered mean-based spread also grows with threshold tightness, from #val("sat_thr_unf_min") to #val("sat_thr_unf_max") km, because that cell weighs more as the footprint shrinks. Top-10%/5#sq footprint membership differs between the two analyses for #val("ev_members_top10_5") NOAA-15 regional records. On the 5#sq grid the median-based maxima are identical with and without QC-1 at every threshold; on the 2#sq grid they differ by at most #val("sat_median2_maxdiff") km.

Neither value is a calibrated inter-satellite difference: the spacecraft differ in local-time sampling and detector history, and a constant intensity factor would not move a percentile footprint or a normalized centroid. Energy-dependent response differences could. Published cross-calibrations for these instruments concern the >16 MeV *integral* channel over earlier decades @lin2019crosscal. They do not apply to the fitted differential fluxes used here, and we apply none.

