= Discussion

== Three different mechanisms

The largest movements reported here arise in different ways, and they should not be ranked against one another. The threshold family moves the centroid by a few hundred kilometres because the percentile *redefines the object*: tighter sets keep fewer, more southerly cells, and their area follows the rule almost exactly. For the top-10% footprint, the channel family moves it by #val("ch_pd_min")–#val("ch_pd_max") km across grids and statistics: the fitted outputs at 25, 50 and 100 MeV place their upper tails in different cells. That ordering held under the day resampling examined here. This study does not separate physical energy structure from the shared fitting model and the channels' response functions. In the unfiltered data, the spacecraft family's largest separation came from *one record* whose flux values do not follow from its own simultaneous >16 MeV count rate. With that record excluded by QC-1, the five spacecraft agree to within #val("sat_max_prim") km at the reference setting, where means and medians give similar values. Short windows add a further effect: they change what can be selected at all.

The ranges of settings are also not commensurate. Five percentiles, three channels, nine windows and five spacecraft were chosen for interpretability, not as samples from comparable distributions of plausible choices. A statement that one factor is "more important" than another would be an artefact of those ranges.

== Implications for particle-based SAA maps

For users of particle-derived SAA locations, four practical points follow. First, report the threshold rule and the population over which it is computed. A percentile over grid cells gives nominal areas by construction, and centroids move non-monotonically as the tail thins. Second, report the eligible support and, when comparing maps, separate end-to-end differences from differences on shared support. Here, most of the three-day end-to-end difference did not persist on shared support, whereas the one-day map still differed there. Reduced support is part of the motivation for multi-spacecraft pooling in short-window studies such as #cite(<xu2025storm>, form: "prose"), although pooling introduces the inter-spacecraft questions discussed above. Third, cell means are exposed to single extreme values. Here one record changed the five-satellite maximum from #val("sat_max_prim") to #val("sat_max_unf") km; a median or a record-level influence check exposes such cases, and a stated rule with the unfiltered result reported alongside keeps any exclusion visible. Fourth, energy channel matters for the thresholded location even where continuous fields overlap substantially.

== Limitations

The analysis covers one month in one solar and geomagnetic context, so it cannot address seasonal, solar-cycle or secular behaviour of the kind studied in @furst2009rxte @schaefer2016saa @qin2014solarcycle @ginisty2024saa. The monthly reference contains every shorter window. Temporal agreement is descriptive, and the paired resampling uses only 31 days, with no established stationarity. Seven-day blocks have 25 possible starts, dates near the month's ends are under-represented, and each join between blocks breaks the dependence between days. The fitted p1–p3 outputs share counts and a spectral model, so channel differences do not come from independent instruments. `mep_IFC_on = −1` is retained without a documented meaning. The processed files carry a provider warning about known contamination, so the data are not certified free of other artefacts. QC-1 was adopted after the influential record had been found, and its parameters were chosen with that knowledge, although the excluded set does not change over wide parameter ranges. It can act only where both neighbourhoods received regular fits, which holds for #val("qc_region_regular")% of regional records; elsewhere (at low count rates, near gaps and at file edges) a single-record artefact would not be detected, and artefacts spanning several records are outside its scope. The level-1a evidence shows an internally inconsistent count-rate vector for the excluded record, not why it arose. No cross-calibration is applied: absolute intensities across spacecraft and channels are not compared, and we cannot exclude energy-dependent response differences as a contributor to spacecraft offsets. The IGRF-derived fields in the files are used only descriptively in the supplement. There, footprint samples sit in the lowest modelled field-strength quartile of each spacecraft's region (Supplement S8). This co-location is descriptive: it does not validate a boundary, and a retired legacy classification is not used. Finally, historical bytes of some raw files used in earlier project checkpoints were not recorded. Regional values were reproduced exactly, but those bytes are not reconstructed.

== Methodology versus physical model

What this work provides is a reproducible methodology: explicit estimands and denominators, end-to-end and shared-support comparisons, a resampling protocol fixed before production resampling, and record-level influence tracing with a stated quality rule and its unfiltered sensitivity, all regenerated from hashed inputs with values drawn from a claims register. It does not provide a validated physical model of the SAA's position or extent, and none of the displacements reported here is a measured motion of the anomaly. The centroid of a percentile footprint is a property of a mapping procedure applied to a sample of fitted fluxes. It becomes physically interpretable only relative to how that procedure responds to its own choices, which is what we have tried to document.

= Conclusions

For January 2024 NOAA/NCEI SEM-2 omnidirectional proton fluxes, a percentile-defined SAA footprint over South America and the South Atlantic is persistent. Its summary location and extent, however, depend on analysis choices by amounts comparable to the separations one might otherwise interpret. Threshold choice moves the centroid by #val("thr_shift_min")–#val("thr_shift_max") km, with an area change that the rule itself prescribes. For the top-10% footprint, channel choice moves it by up to about #val("ch_pd_max") km, with the p3-versus-p1 direction persisting under paired day resampling. For three days, most of the end-to-end disagreement with the monthly map does not persist on shared eligible support, whereas a one-day map still differs there. With one NOAA-15 record excluded by a stated post-hoc rule, the five-satellite spread is #val("sat_max_prim") km (#val("sat_max_median_prim") km for cell medians); without the rule it is #val("sat_max_unf") km for cell means. Reporting the selection population, eligible support, estimator and influential observations alongside any particle-derived SAA location would make such maps comparable across studies.

= Data and code availability

Input data are the public NOAA/NCEI processed SEM-2 daily files @ncei_sem2 for 1–31 January 2024 (five spacecraft); file names and SHA-256 hashes are listed in the supplement's input manifest. One level-1a companion file from the same archive (`poes_n15_20240119_raw.nc`) was read only as evidence for the excluded record; its hash is listed there too. Analysis code, the frozen resampling protocol, derived tables, the claims register, figure-generation code and these document sources are in the project repository `fellipegoncalvesleite/saa-poes-mapping`. At the time of writing, this final version exists as a local, uncommitted working tree with a content-hashed source snapshot; it has not been published to the repository's main branch, and no archival DOI has been assigned. A single reproduction command (`python scripts/reproduce_paper.py`) regenerates all reported values from the raw files.

